(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the physical and chemical genesis of soil horizons within a mature soil profile and discuss the use of the USDA soil textural triangle.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Soil Genesis, Master Horizons, and Textural Classification:) Tj
T*
(   1. Pedogenesis and Master Soil Horizons:) Tj
T*
(   Soil is a dynamic natural body formed by weathering of parent rocks under the influence of) Tj
T*
(   climate, organisms, relief, and time. A mature soil profile exhibits distinct genetic layers:) Tj
T*
(   - O Horizon \(Organic Layer\): Surface layer rich in fresh and decomposing organic matter \(litter) Tj
T*
(   and duff\).) Tj
T*
(   - A Horizon \(Topsoil\): Mineral layer containing dark, decomposed humus, high microbial) Tj
T*
(   activity, and primary root growth.) Tj
T*
(   - E Horizon \(Eluvial / Leached Layer\): Pale, bleached horizon characterized by intense leaching) Tj
T*
(   of clays, iron, and aluminum oxides leaving resistant quartz.) Tj
T*
(   - B Horizon \(Subsoil / Illuvial Layer\): Zone of accumulation where leached clays, silicate) Tj
T*
(   minerals, and sesquioxides \(Fe2O3, Al2O3\) precipitate.) Tj
T*
(   - C Horizon \(Substratum\): Weathered parent material, minimally altered by biological) Tj
T*
(   pedogenesis.) Tj
T*
(   - R Horizon \(Bedrock\): Unweathered consolidated bedrock \(granite, basalt, limestone\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Soil Texture and the USDA Textural Triangle:) Tj
T*
(   - Soil texture is determined by the relative mass proportions of three mineral particle) Tj
T*
(   fractions: Sand \(0.05 to 2.0 mm\), Silt \(0.002 to 0.05 mm\), and Clay \(< 0.002 mm\).) Tj
T*
(   - The USDA Textural Triangle has 12 textural classes \(e.g., Loam, Clay Loam, Sandy Clay, Silty) Tj
T*
(   Clay\). Loams, possessing balanced proportions of sand, silt, and clay, provide optimal) Tj
T*
(   hydraulic conductivity, aeration, and nutrient retention for vegetation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 8:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the chemical origins of Soil Cation Exchange Capacity \(CEC\) and contrast permanent isomorphous substitution charges with pH-dependent surface charges.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Mechanisms and Chemical Origins of Cation Exchange Capacity \(CEC\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition of CEC:) Tj
T*
(   Cation Exchange Capacity is the total capacity of a soil to hold exchangeable cations,) Tj
T*
(   expressed in centimoles of positive charge per kilogram of dry soil \(cmol_c/kg\) or) Tj
T*
(   milliequivalents per 100 grams \(meq/100g\). It governs soil fertility, fertilizer retention, and) Tj
T*
(   buffer capacity.) Tj
T*
(   2. Permanent \(Structural\) Charge via Isomorphous Substitution:) Tj
T*
(   - During crystalline aluminosilicate clay synthesis, a cation of similar ionic radius but lower) Tj
T*
(   valence substitutes into the lattice without altering crystal geometry.) Tj
T*
(   - Tetrahedral Sheet: Al3+ substitutes for Si4+, generating one net negative surface charge.) Tj
T*
(   - Octahedral Sheet: Mg2+ or Fe2+ substitutes for Al3+, creating permanent negative lattice) Tj
T*
(   sites.) Tj
T*
(   - These charges are permanent, invariant with soil solution pH, and predominate in 2:1) Tj
T*
(   expanding clays \(Montmorillonite/Smectite: CEC = 80-150 meq/100g; Vermiculite: CEC = 100-200) Tj
T*
(   meq/100g\).) Tj
T*
(   3. pH-Dependent \(Variable\) Charge:) Tj
T*
(   - Originates at broken crystal edges, surface hydroxyl groups \(-Al-OH, -Fe-OH\), and) Tj
T*
