(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the chemical distinctions between Saline, Sodic, and Saline-Sodic soils, explaining how Sodium Adsorption Ratio \(SAR\) and Exchangeable Sodium Percentage \(ESP\) govern soil dispersion.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Diagnosis and Mechanics of Saline, Sodic, and Saline-Sodic Soils:) Tj
T*
(   1. Diagnostic Classification Criteria:) Tj
T*
(   - Saline Soil: Electrical Conductivity \(EC\) >= 4.0 dS/m; Exchangeable Sodium Percentage \(ESP\) <) Tj
T*
(   15%; Sodium Adsorption Ratio \(SAR\) < 13; Soil pH generally < 8.5. Dominated by neutral soluble) Tj
T*
(   salts \(NaCl, Na2SO4, CaCl2, MgSO4\).) Tj
T*
(   - Sodic Soil: EC < 4.0 dS/m; ESP >= 15%; SAR >= 13; Soil pH typically 8.5 to 10.0 \(due to) Tj
T*
(   Na2CO3 / NaHCO3 hydrolysis\). High exchangeable Na+ on colloids.) Tj
T*
(   - Saline-Sodic Soil: EC >= 4.0 dS/m; ESP >= 15%; SAR >= 13; Variable pH \(typically <= 8.5 due) Tj
T*
(   to high ionic strength suppressing alkaline hydrolysis\).) Tj
T*
(   2. The Diffuse Double Layer \(DDL\) and Dispersion Mechanics:) Tj
T*
