(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the thermo-chemical synthesis, structural morphology, and biogeochemical carbon sequestration mechanisms of Biochar.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Biochar Synthesis, Physicochemical Morphology, and Carbon Sequestration:) Tj
T*
(   1. Thermo-Chemical Pyrolysis Regimes:) Tj
T*
(   Biochar is the solid carbonaceous residue produced by the thermochemical decomposition of) Tj
T*
(   biomass feedstocks \(crop residues, wood chips, forestry slash\) under limited or zero oxygen) Tj
T*
(   supply:) Tj
T*
(   - Slow Pyrolysis: Heating rate of 5 to 30 deg C/min, peak temperature 450 to 600 deg C,) Tj
T*
(   residence time 30 to 120 minutes. Maximizes biochar yield \(30-40% by dry mass\) with optimal) Tj
T*
(   porous structure.) Tj
T*
(   - Contrast with Fast Pyrolysis: Rapid heating \(> 1000 deg C/s\) and short vapor residence \(< 2) Tj
T*
(   s\), which optimizes liquid bio-oil yield \(60-75%\) at the expense of solid char.) Tj
T*
(   2. Structural and Surface Chemistry:) Tj
T*
(   - Dehydration and condensation reactions strip off volatile oxygen and hydrogen, transforming) Tj
T*
(   aliphatic cellulose and lignin polymers into condensed polyaromatic graphene sheets.) Tj
T*
(   - Scanning electron microscopy \(SEM\) reveals a highly porous honeycomb network mirroring the) Tj
T*
(   vascular tracheid and xylem morphology of the parent plant.) Tj
T*
(   - The outer surfaces develop abundant oxygen-containing functional groups \(-COOH, -OH, C=O\)) Tj
T*
(   during progressive surface weathering, providing high cation exchange capacity \(CEC\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Carbon Sequestration and Soil Amelioration:) Tj
T*
(   - Biochar carbon has a mean residence time of several centuries to millennia in soils due to) Tj
T*
(   the resistance of fused aromatic ring structures against microbial enzymatic cleavage \(O:C) Tj
T*
(   atomic ratio < 0.2, H:C atomic ratio < 0.4\).) Tj
T*
(   - Enhances soil water holding capacity, reduces fertilizer nutrient leaching, immobilizes heavy) Tj
T*
(   metals through surface complexation, and acts as a stable sink for atmospheric CO2.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
