BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Elucidate the four primary mechanisms of Phytoremediation, and explain the biological processes governing Phytoextraction and Phytostabilization.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Mechanisms of Phytoremediation: Phytoextraction, Phytostabilization, and Beyond:) Tj
T*
(   1. Primary Phytoremediation Paradigms:) Tj
T*
(   Phytoremediation is the in-situ, solar-driven remediation technology that employs green plants) Tj
T*
(   and their associated rhizosphere microorganisms to contain, sequester, or detoxify) Tj
T*
(   environmental contaminants.) Tj
T*
(   - Phytoextraction \(Phytoaccumulation\): Root uptake of contaminants \(heavy metals,) Tj
T*
(   radionuclides, metalloids\) from soil, translocating and concentrating them into harvestable) Tj
T*
(   aerial biomass \(stems and leaves\). Harvested biomass is incinerated or processed for) Tj
T*
(   phytomining.) Tj
T*
(   - Phytostabilization: Plants immobilize toxic contaminants in the rhizosphere through root) Tj
T*
(   adsorption, complexation with root mucilage, or precipitation in the soil matrix, rendering) Tj
T*
(   them bio-unavailable and preventing groundwater leaching.) Tj
T*
(   - Rhizofiltration: Hydroponically cultivated plant roots absorb, precipitate, and concentrate) Tj
T*
(   heavy metals from aqueous waste streams.) Tj
T*
(   - Phytodegradation / Phytotransformation: Plants enzymatically metabolize organic pollutants) Tj
T*
(   \(pesticides, chlorinated solvents, explosive residues\) inside plant tissues using cytochrome) Tj
T*
(   P450 monooxygenases, peroxidases, and glutathione S-transferases.) Tj
T*
(   2. Molecular Physiology of Phytoextraction:) Tj
T*
(   - Mobilization: Root exudation of phytosiderophores and low-molecular-weight organic acids) Tj
T*
(   \(citric, oxalic, malic\) mobilizes tightly bound metal cations from soil colloids.) Tj
T*
